                  "The landlord levies on a natural-person former tenant's bank account or other personal property under an execution.",
                  "The levy is made by serving the execution on the garnishee (bank, employer, other holder), or by seizure of property capable of delivery. It binds property and debts then held or later coming due until transferred to the enforcement officer, and is void 90 days after service except as to what was transferred or is in a CPLR 5225/5227 turnover proceeding, unless the court extends it on the creditor's motion. The officer mails or delivers to the tenant, within four days after serving a garnishee, the execution with the CPLR 5222(e) exemption notice unless it was served within the prior year, to the residence, else in a 'personal and confidential' envelope to the workplace, else any known address. On a bank account: $2,500 is untouchable where identifiable exempt payments were deposited in the prior 45 days, plus the minimum-wage-based amount; the exemption notice and two claim forms are served with the execution; the bank holds the funds at least 27 days and may transfer after 30 days if no claim form or order arrives; no bank fee where the account holds only exempt funds. A creditor that names in its notice property or a debt belonging to someone other than the tenant is liable to that person for damages from the levy.",
